    "quic_unsupported.txt": "# Hosts whose endpoint serves no HTTP/3 at all, so test 2 does not probe their\n# QUIC column: the cell prints a dash, the row keeps its TCP verdicts, and the\n# summary counts the QUIC column out of the hosts that can answer it instead of\n# the whole list.\n#\n# The rule is the two third-party testers agreeing on \"no HTTP/3\"\n# (`scripts/quic/online_h3.py` — intodns.ai and http3check.net, probing from\n# networks that are not ours; run 2026-10-02). A row where they disagree stays\n# probed: the disagreement is exactly the question this column asks.\n#\n# Caveat, recorded in scripts/quic/README.md: five of these hosts\n# (aws.amazon.com, soundcloud.com, vk.ru, www.currenttime.tv, www.svoboda.org)\n# do answer a handshake when one is forced on them — they just never advertise\n# HTTP/3, and a browser that is never told does not use it. They stay listed.\n#\n# Regenerate the data with:  python scripts/quic/online_h3.py\n#\n# One host per line, `#` starts a comment, the host is matched whole\n# (case-insensitive) against the domain's host part.\naws.amazon.com\nbrowserleaks.com\nprotonvpn.com\nshikimori.io\nsoundcloud.com\nvk.ru\nwww.canva.com\nwww.cdn77.com\nwww.coursera.org\nwww.currenttime.tv\nwww.linuxserver.io\nwww.svoboda.org\nwww.themoscowtimes.com\nwww.torproject.org\n"
